Q: Recording Time in C++ with Chrono A: <p><a href="https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/chrono/time_point.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>std::chrono::time_point</code></a> is not a concrete type but a <strong>template</strong>.<br />
This is the cause of the error you get when returning it.</p>
<p>Instead you should specify the template argument to make it a concrete type:</p>
<pre><code>//---------------------vvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvv---------------
std::chrono::time_point&lt;std::chrono::system_clock&gt; timeFunction()
{
    return std::chrono::system_clock::now();
}
</code></pre>
<p>Recent compilers support deducing the return type with <code>auto</code> which makes it simpler:</p>
<pre><code>auto timeFunction()
{
    return std::chrono::system_clock::now();
}
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/8q7fYbbWf" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Live demo and usage example.</a></p>

Q: Save data to Arduino for analyzing later A: <p>Some microcontrollers, such as the Uno, have a type of memory called EEPROM. The Arduino Uno has <a href="https://docs.arduino.cc/learn/programming/eeprom-guide/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">512 bytes of EEPROM </a>(more on some boards), which, if you use 8 bit integers, is 512 data points.</p>
<p>If you're measuring every minute, that's 8 1/2 hours of data. EEPROM is nice because it doesn't reset when the board resets. Here's an example of something you could do:</p>
<pre class="lang-cc prettyprint-override"><code>// snip...

#include &lt;EEPROM.h&gt;

void print_values();

void setup() {
  // snip...
  print_values();
}

uint16_t write_addr = 0

void loop() {
  // snip...
  double precise_temp = mlx.readObjectTempF();
  // ...print precise temp to serial...

  // Discard decimals and convert to signed integer
  int8_t storeable_temp = static_cast&lt;int8_t&gt;(precise_temp);

  // If write address is above maximum, do nothing and print error to serial
  // Otherwise, write at EEPROM address and increment
  if (write_addr &gt;= EEPROM.length())
    Serial.println(&quot;Data Overflowed!&quot;);
  else {
    EEPROM.write(write_addr, storeable_temp);
    write_addr++;
  }

  delay(1000);
}


// Prints all of stored EEPROM in CSV format
void print_values() {
  Serial.println(&quot;---&quot;);
  Serial.println(&quot;addr,temp&quot;);
  for (int i = 0; i &lt; EEPROM.length(); i++) {
    Serial.print(i);
    Serial.print(&quot;,&quot;);
    int8_t temp = static_cast&lt;int8_t&gt;(EEPROM.read(i));
    Serial.println(temp);
  }
  Serial.println(&quot;---&quot;);
}
</code></pre>
<p>Unfortunately using 8 bit integers limits the precision of the result. If you wanted to you could use some other sort of data type at the cost of storage space. To get the values from the board, you can connect a serial monitor. The Uno's Auto-Reset system will restart the program and the values will be printed to serial.</p>
<p>You may want to use an EEPROM address to store what index the writer was last at, for knowing how much data you actually collected vs what was garbage from last time, and so that the program doesn't overwrite the data each time it is power cycled.</p>
